{
  "id": "loss-factors-australia",
  "label": "Retail and consumer losses aligned to Australian measurement (FIAL 2021)",
  "line": "improved",
  "description": "The thesis's retail loss (Base!I, Buzby 2010) and consumer loss (Base!G, Muth 2011) are US figures. Retail: replaced, category by category, by the Australian figure: the retail waste measured by the National Food Waste Strategy Feasibility Study (FIAL 2021, 2018-19, Table 5) as a share of the Australian supply of that food group (FAO food balance, mean 2018 and 2019). Each food keeps its share of its category's loss, so the thesis's differences between foods are preserved and only the level changes. Targets for the share of purchased mass lost at retail: Cereal whole 0.5%, Cereal ref 0.5%, Dairy High 0.7%, Dairy Med 0.7%, Dairy Low 0.7%, Fruit 3.2%, Meat red 1.9%, Meat white 3.3%, Nut & seed 0.5%, Vege green 3.0%, Vege orange 3.0%, Vege other 3.0%, Vege starch 3.0%. Categories with no FIAL group (legumes, fats and oils, other, lacto-ovo) are unchanged. Consumer: the total is aligned to FIAL's consumption-stage waste, 24.6% of the food reaching those stages (3,932 thousand tonnes: households 2,463, hospitality 1,218, procurement 251), against 21.6% in the thesis, and the differences between food groups are taken from the one direct measurement of household waste by food type, the Burnside (South Australia) kerbside bin audit of 2022 (175 households, three bins each, weighed by food type): waste in the bins over the ABS retail sales of the same foods per household gives meat, poultry and fish about 15%, vegetables 30%, fruit 38%, bread and pasta 34%. Only the differences between these four groups are used (their weighted mean is kept at the thesis's level); the other groups (dairy, nuts, legumes, fats, other) keep the thesis's shape, and one factor then brings the total to the FIAL figure. FIAL's own tonnes by group over the ABS sales give the same pattern for meat (14% for red meat, 21% for poultry) and the same capacity (+11.3% against +11.0%), which is the cross-check. The primary-to-retail loss (Base!L) is unchanged: FIAL's primary and processing stages are not comparable with it. The avoidable waste setting (Settings!B14) still scales the three losses together, so its default of 12% now means Australian measured waste.",
